{"slug":"deploy-apps","title":"Deploy apps into your namespaces, one at a time","description":"Run an app in each namespace, place pods on chosen nodes, and reach what you deployed.","order":6,"tags":["kubectl","namespaces"],"date":"2026-10-14","minutes":13,"words":1354,"toc":[{"depth":2,"id":"part-1--deploy-one-app-at-a-time","text":"Part 1 — Deploy, one app at a time"},{"depth":3,"id":"11-web--nginx-two-copies-in-namespace-web","text":"1.1 web — nginx, two copies, in namespace web"},{"depth":3,"id":"12-cache--redis-in-namespace-cache","text":"1.2 cache — Redis, in namespace cache"},{"depth":3,"id":"13-db--postgres-pinned-to-the-data-node","text":"1.3 db — Postgres, pinned to the data node"},{"depth":3,"id":"14-a-single-bare-pod","text":"1.4 A single bare pod"},{"depth":3,"id":"15-pin-a-pod-to-a-specific-node-by-name","text":"1.5 Pin a pod to a specific node by name"},{"depth":3,"id":"16-see-everything","text":"1.6 See everything"},{"depth":2,"id":"part-2--use-what-you-deployed","text":"Part 2 — Use what you deployed"},{"depth":2,"id":"where-next","text":"Where next"}],"html":"<p>Run an app in each namespace, place pods on chosen nodes, and use what you deployed. This assumes the cluster\nfrom <a href=\"/blog/kind-cluster\">Create a local Kubernetes cluster with kind</a> (or <a href=\"/blog/k3d-cluster\">Create a local Kubernetes cluster with k3d</a>)\nis running, <code>kubectl config current-context</code> shows it, and the namespaces <code>web</code>, <code>cache</code> and <code>db</code> exist.\nThe commands are the same on kind and k3d, except where a note says otherwise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-1--deploy-one-app-at-a-time\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#part-1--deploy-one-app-at-a-time\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Part 1 — Deploy, one app at a time</h2>\n<p>Each app below goes into its own namespace. A <strong>Deployment</strong> is the standard way to run an app: you say\n&quot;keep N copies of this image running&quot;, and Kubernetes creates the pods and replaces any that die.</p>\n<h3 id=\"11-web--nginx-two-copies-in-namespace-web\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#11-web--nginx-two-copies-in-namespace-web\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.1 <code>web</code> — nginx, two copies, in namespace <code>web</code></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20create%20deployment%20web%20--image%3Dnginx%3A1.27%20--replicas%3D2%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=2 -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><code>deployment web</code> — the Deployment&#39;s name (its pods are named <code>web-&lt;hash&gt;-&lt;hash&gt;</code>).</li>\n<li><code>--image=nginx:1.27</code> — what to run. Always pin a version rather than <code>latest</code>; <a href=\"/blog/run-your-image\">Run your own image in the cluster</a> shows why.</li>\n<li><code>--replicas=2</code> — two pods.</li>\n<li><code>-n web</code> — in the <code>web</code> namespace.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wait for it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20rollout%20status%20deployment%2Fweb%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/web -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Prints <code>successfully rolled out</code>. Now see where the scheduler put the pods:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20get%20pods%20-n%20web%20-o%20wide%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl get pods -n web -o wide</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The <code>NODE</code> column shows each pod&#39;s node. With no instructions the scheduler spreads them over the workers.</p>\n<p>Give the pods a stable name inside the cluster with a <strong>Service</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20expose%20deployment%20web%20--port%3D80%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20get%20service%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl get service -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>web</code> with a <code>CLUSTER-IP</code>. Other pods can now reach nginx at <code>web</code> (same namespace) or <code>web.web</code>\n(<code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;</code>, from any namespace).</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-cache--redis-in-namespace-cache\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#12-cache--redis-in-namespace-cache\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.2 <code>cache</code> — Redis, in namespace <code>cache</code></h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20create%20deployment%20cache%20--image%3Dredis%3A7-alpine%20-n%20cache%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl create deployment cache --image=redis:7-alpine -n cache</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20rollout%20status%20deployment%2Fcache%20-n%20cache%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/cache -n cache</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20expose%20deployment%20cache%20--port%3D6379%20-n%20cache%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl expose deployment cache --port=6379 -n cache</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Prove it works by running a command inside the pod:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20exec%20-n%20cache%20deploy%2Fcache%20--%20redis-cli%20ping%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> -n cache deploy/cache -- redis-cli ping</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Prints <code>PONG</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"13-db--postgres-pinned-to-the-data-node\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#13-db--postgres-pinned-to-the-data-node\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.3 <code>db</code> — Postgres, pinned to the <code>data</code> node</h3>\n<p>This one is placed deliberately. A YAML manifest lets you say more than the one-line <code>create</code> can. The\n<code>nodeSelector</code> below means &quot;only on a node labelled <code>role=data</code>&quot; (Part 4.3 of <a href=\"/blog/kind-cluster\">Create a local Kubernetes cluster with kind</a> labelled <code>worker2</code> that way):</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20apply%20-f%20-%20%3C%3C'EOF'%22%2C%22apiVersion%3A%20apps%2Fv1%22%2C%22kind%3A%20Deployment%22%2C%22metadata%3A%22%2C%22%20%20name%3A%20db%22%2C%22%20%20namespace%3A%20db%22%2C%22spec%3A%22%2C%22%20%20replicas%3A%201%22%2C%22%20%20selector%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20matchLabels%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20app%3A%20db%22%2C%22%20%20template%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20metadata%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20labels%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20app%3A%20db%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20spec%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20nodeSelector%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20role%3A%20data%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20containers%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20-%20name%3A%20postgres%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20image%3A%20postgres%3A16-alpine%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20env%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20-%20name%3A%20POSTGRES_PASSWORD%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20value%3A%20local-only%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20ports%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20-%20containerPort%3A%205432%22%2C%22EOF%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;</span>\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: db\n  namespace: db\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: db\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: db\n    spec:\n      nodeSelector:\n        role: data\n      containers:\n        - name: postgres\n          image: postgres:16-alpine\n          <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">env</span>:\n            - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD\n              value: local-only\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 5432\nEOF</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><code>kubectl apply -f -</code> — create or update whatever the YAML describes; <code>-</code> means &quot;read it from the lines\nthat follow&quot;.</li>\n<li><code>selector.matchLabels</code> / <code>template.metadata.labels</code> — how the Deployment recognises its own pods. The two\nmust match.</li>\n<li><code>nodeSelector</code> — the placement rule.</li>\n<li><code>env</code> — a throwaway password. Real secrets belong in a Kubernetes Secret, not in YAML you commit.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20rollout%20status%20deployment%2Fdb%20-n%20db%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/db -n db</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20expose%20deployment%20db%20--port%3D5432%20-n%20db%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl expose deployment db --port=5432 -n db</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20exec%20-n%20db%20deploy%2Fdb%20--%20psql%20-U%20postgres%20-c%20'select%20version()'%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> -n db deploy/db -- psql -U postgres -c <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select version()&#x27;</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Prints the Postgres version.</p>\n<h3 id=\"14-a-single-bare-pod\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#14-a-single-bare-pod\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.4 A single bare pod</h3>\n<p>A Deployment is for apps that must stay up. For a quick one-off, run a plain pod:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20run%20toolbox%20--image%3Dbusybox%3A1.36%20-n%20web%20--restart%3DNever%20--command%20--%20sleep%203600%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl run toolbox --image=busybox:1.36 -n web --restart=Never --<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">command</span> -- <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep</span> 3600</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>--restart=Never</code> makes it a plain pod: if it dies, nothing recreates it. <code>sleep 3600</code> keeps it alive for an\nhour so you can work inside it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"15-pin-a-pod-to-a-specific-node-by-name\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#15-pin-a-pod-to-a-specific-node-by-name\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.5 Pin a pod to a specific node by name</h3>\n<p>Besides labels, every node has a built-in <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> label:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20apply%20-f%20-%20%3C%3C'EOF'%22%2C%22apiVersion%3A%20v1%22%2C%22kind%3A%20Pod%22%2C%22metadata%3A%22%2C%22%20%20name%3A%20pinned%22%2C%22%20%20namespace%3A%20web%22%2C%22spec%3A%22%2C%22%20%20nodeSelector%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20kubernetes.io%2Fhostname%3A%20local-cluster-1-worker%22%2C%22%20%20containers%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20-%20name%3A%20nginx%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20image%3A%20nginx%3A1.27%22%2C%22EOF%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;</span>\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: pinned\n  namespace: web\nspec:\n  nodeSelector:\n    kubernetes.io/hostname: local-cluster-1-worker\n  containers:\n    - name: nginx\n      image: nginx:1.27\nEOF</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"16-see-everything\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#16-see-everything\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1.6 See everything</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20get%20pods%20-A%20-o%20wide%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl get pods -A -o wide</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>-A</code> means all namespaces. Find your pods and their <code>NODE</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>web-…</code> ×2 (spread over both workers), plus <code>toolbox</code> and <code>pinned</code> (<code>pinned</code> on <code>local-cluster-1-worker</code>)</li>\n<li><code>cache-…</code> on a worker</li>\n<li><code>db-…</code> on <code>local-cluster-1-worker2</code>, because of <code>role=data</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Or just one folder at a time:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20get%20all%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl get all -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> every pod is <code>Running</code> and <code>READY</code> shows <code>1/1</code> (give the first image pulls a minute).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"part-2--use-what-you-deployed\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#part-2--use-what-you-deployed\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Part 2 — Use what you deployed</h2>\n<p><strong>2.1 Open nginx from your Mac with a port-forward.</strong> This tunnels a Mac port to a Service. It stays in the\nforeground, so use a second terminal tab for the <code>curl</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20port-forward%20svc%2Fweb%208081%3A80%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl port-forward svc/web 8081:80 -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<p>In the other tab:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22curl%20-s%20http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8081%20%7C%20head%20-5%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>curl -s http://localhost:8081 | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">head</span> -5</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The start of the nginx welcome page. Stop the tunnel with <code>Ctrl-C</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>2.2 Reach one namespace from another.</strong> Run a short-lived pod in <code>cache</code> that fetches the page from the\n<code>web</code> Service:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20run%20dns-test%20-n%20cache%20--rm%20-i%20--restart%3DNever%20--image%3Dbusybox%3A1.36%20--%20wget%20-qO-%20http%3A%2F%2Fweb.web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl run dns-test -n cache --<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm</span> -i --restart=Never --image=busybox:1.36 -- wget -qO- http://web.web</code></pre></figure>\n<p>In one sentence: it starts a temporary pod in <code>cache</code>, uses it to fetch a page from the <code>web</code> Service in another\nnamespace, prints the result, and deletes the pod.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table (scrolls sideways)\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>kubectl run dns-test</code></td><td>Create a single pod named <code>dns-test</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-n cache</code></td><td>Put it in the <code>cache</code> namespace, so the request comes from there</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>--image=busybox:1.36</code></td><td>The container image: a tiny Linux toolbox that includes <code>wget</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>--restart=Never</code></td><td>A plain one-off pod: run once, do not restart when it finishes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>--rm</code></td><td>Delete the pod when the command ends</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-i</code></td><td>Keep stdin attached and wait for the command, so its output shows in your terminal</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>--</code></td><td>Everything after this is the command to run inside the pod, not options for kubectl</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>wget -qO- http://web.web</code></td><td>The command the pod runs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The command inside the pod:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>wget</code> downloads a URL.</li>\n<li><code>-q</code> is quiet (no progress output).</li>\n<li><code>-O-</code> writes the page to standard output instead of a file, so it appears on your screen.</li>\n<li><code>http://web.web</code> is <code>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;</code>: the Service <code>web</code> in the namespace <code>web</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What it proves.</strong> The pod is in <code>cache</code> but reaches a Service in <code>web</code>. Cluster DNS turns <code>web.web</code> into the\nService&#39;s address, so a pod in one namespace can call a Service in another. The output is the nginx welcome\npage HTML. Run from inside the <code>web</code> namespace, plain <code>web</code> would work too, because the namespace part\ndefaults to the pod&#39;s own.</p>\n<p>A warning &quot;couldn&#39;t attach to pod&quot; is harmless: the pod finished before kubectl attached.</p>\n<p><strong>2.3 Reach a Service without port-forward (NodePort).</strong> This reaches <code>web</code> from your local machine at\n<code>localhost:8080</code>, with no command left running.</p>\n<p><strong>The problem.</strong> A normal Service (ClusterIP) is reachable only from inside the cluster, and kind&#39;s nodes are\nDocker containers, so their ports are not open on your local machine either. A request has to cross two\nboundaries:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Local machine to node container.</strong> This is what <code>extraPortMappings</code> in Part 2.1 of <a href=\"/blog/kind-cluster\">Create a local Kubernetes cluster with kind</a> set up: local port\n<code>8080</code> goes to port <code>30080</code> on the control-plane container.</li>\n<li><strong>Node to pod.</strong> This is what a <code>NodePort</code> Service sets up: port <code>30080</code> on every node forwards to the\nService&#39;s pods.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Create the Service:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20apply%20-f%20-%20%3C%3C'EOF'%22%2C%22apiVersion%3A%20v1%22%2C%22kind%3A%20Service%22%2C%22metadata%3A%22%2C%22%20%20name%3A%20web-public%22%2C%22%20%20namespace%3A%20web%22%2C%22spec%3A%22%2C%22%20%20type%3A%20NodePort%22%2C%22%20%20selector%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20app%3A%20web%22%2C%22%20%20ports%3A%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20-%20port%3A%2080%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20targetPort%3A%2080%22%2C%22%20%20%20%20%20%20nodePort%3A%2030080%22%2C%22EOF%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl apply -f - &lt;&lt;<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;</span>\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: web-public\n  namespace: web\nspec:\n  <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">type</span>: NodePort\n  selector:\n    app: web\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 80\n      nodePort: 30080\nEOF</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22curl%20-s%20http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8080%20%7C%20head%20-5%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>curl -s http://localhost:8080 | <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">head</span> -5</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The nginx page again, no tunnel running.</p>\n<p><strong>The YAML, field by field</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table (scrolls sideways)\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>name: web-public</code>, <code>namespace: web</code></td><td>A second Service next to the existing <code>web</code> one, in the same namespace as the pods</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>type: NodePort</code></td><td>In addition to a normal cluster address, open a fixed port on every node</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>selector: app: web</code></td><td>Send traffic to pods labelled <code>app=web</code>; <code>kubectl create deployment web</code> labelled its pods that way</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>port: 80</code></td><td>The Service&#39;s own port, used inside the cluster</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>targetPort: 80</code></td><td>The port the nginx container listens on</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>nodePort: 30080</code></td><td>The port opened on each node. It must match the <code>containerPort: 30080</code> in <code>kind-config.yaml</code> and be in the range 30000–32767</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The path a request takes</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">text</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>curl localhost:8080\n  → local machine, port 8080\n  → control-plane container, port 30080     (extraPortMappings)\n  → Service web-public                      (NodePort)\n  → one of the web pods, port 80</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Why it works through the control-plane node.</strong> The <code>extraPortMappings</code> entry is on the control-plane node,\nbut the pods run on the workers. This still works because kube-proxy opens the node port on <strong>every</strong> node,\nnot only the ones running the pods. Traffic arriving at the control-plane&#39;s port <code>30080</code> is forwarded across\nthe cluster to a <code>web</code> pod on whichever worker has one.</p>\n<p><strong>Compared with <code>port-forward</code> (2.1)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table (scrolls sideways)\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>kubectl port-forward</code></th><th>NodePort + <code>extraPortMappings</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Needs a command left running</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Set up when</td><td>any time</td><td>the port mapping must exist when the cluster is created</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spreads traffic over pods</td><td>no, it picks one pod</td><td>yes, across all of them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>If it does not answer:</strong> check that the cluster was created with the <code>extraPortMappings</code> block, that\n<code>kubectl get svc -n web</code> shows <code>30080</code> for <code>web-public</code>, and that <code>kubectl get endpoints web-public -n web</code>\nlists pod IPs (an empty list means the selector matches nothing).</p>\n<p><strong>2.4 Logs and a shell:</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20logs%20deploy%2Fweb%20-n%20web%20--tail%3D5%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl logs deploy/web -n web --<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail</span>=5</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20exec%20-it%20toolbox%20-n%20web%20--%20sh%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> -it toolbox -n web -- sh</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A shell inside the <code>toolbox</code> pod (<code>exit</code> to leave).</p>\n<p><strong>2.5 Watch Kubernetes heal itself.</strong> In one tab:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20get%20pods%20-n%20web%20-w%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl get pods -n web -w</code></pre></figure>\n<p>In another, delete one of the <code>web</code> pods (copy a real name from the list):</p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl delete pod &lt;web-pod-name&gt; -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The Deployment immediately starts a replacement. The bare pods <code>toolbox</code> and <code>pinned</code> would not come back.\n<code>Ctrl-C</code> ends the watch.</p>\n<p><strong>2.6 Scale up and down:</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20scale%20deployment%20web%20--replicas%3D4%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=4 -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><figcaption><span class=\"lang\">bash</span><a class=\"code-try\" href=\"/app?run=%5B%22kubectl%20scale%20deployment%20web%20--replicas%3D2%20-n%20web%22%5D&amp;notour=1\">Try in playground</a><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></figcaption><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=2 -n web</code></pre></figure>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"where-next\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#where-next\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Where next</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Run an image you built yourself: <a href=\"/blog/run-your-image\">Run your own image in the cluster</a></li>\n<li>Stop or remove it all: <a href=\"/blog/clean-up\">Stop, shut down and clean up</a></li>\n</ul>\n"}